TypeScript supports Duck Typing through interfaces, which define the expected shape of an object, without enforcing a specific class or type. Interfaces allow developers to write more flexible and reusable code, while still providing type safety and tooling support. By using interfaces, TypeScript encourages the use of Duck Typing, which can make code more readable and maintainable, especially when dealing with third-party libraries or external APIs.

